      "new_string": "### 4. Blocking Port (Detailed Explanation)\n\n#### What Gets Blocked?\nAny port that is **not** a Root Port and **not** a Designated Port is placed in a **Blocking** (or Discarding) state. In a network with redundant links, these blocked ports are the \"spare\" paths that could create loops if they were active.\n\n#### How Does a Port Become Blocked?\n\nThe blocking decision is made through **BPDU comparison** at each switch. Here's the step-by-step process:\n\n1. **Every port receives BPDUs** from neighboring switches (or forwards them from the Root Bridge).\n2. **The switch compares BPDUs** using a strict hierarchy of criteria:\n   - **Root Path Cost** (lowest wins): The cumulative cost to reach the Root Bridge.\n   - **Sender Bridge ID** (lowest wins): If path costs tie, the switch with the lower Bridge ID.\n   - **Port ID** (lowest wins): If sender BIDs tie, the port number with lower priority.\n\n3. **Port roles are assigned** based on the comparison:\n   - On a **non-root switch**: The port with the **best BPDU** (lowest cost/BID/port ID) becomes the **Root Port**.\n   - On each **network segment** (link between two switches): The port on the switch closest to the Root (lowest total cost) becomes the **Designated Port**.\n   - Any other port is placed in the **Blocking state**.\n\n#### The BPDU Comparison in Action\n\nExample: Suppose there are three switches (A, B, C) forming a triangle, with A as the Root Bridge.\n\n```\n        A (Root)\n       / \\\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n      \\   /\n       \\ /\n```\n\n**For Switch B:**\n- Receives BPDU from A on port 1 with cost 4 → **This becomes the Root Port** (lowest cost to root).\n- On the link B↔C: B compares its BPDU (cost 8 to root via A) with C's BPDU (cost 8 to root via A).\n  - If B's Bridge ID is lower than C's, B's port becomes the **Designated Port** on that link.\n  - If C's Bridge ID is lower, B's port on that link goes to **Blocking state**.\n\n#### What Happens on a Blocked Port?\n\n**Blocked ports:**\n- **Do NOT forward user data** (frames destined to/from end-devices are discarded).\n- **Do NOT learn MAC addresses** from traffic on the segment.\n- **DO listen to and process BPDUs** to monitor the network topology.\n- Remain in a **\"ready but inactive\"** state, waiting for a topology change.\n\nThis is why frames entering a blocked port are immediately discarded—the port has no role in the active spanning tree.\n\n#### State Transitions: From Blocking to Forwarding\n\nWhen a port is first assigned the blocking role, it enters the **Blocking state** (STP) or **Discarding state** (RSTP/newer standards). If a topology change occurs (e.g., a link or switch fails), a blocked port can transition to forwarding:\n\n1. **Blocking** (listens to BPDUs only) → **20 seconds max** →\n2. **Listening** (prepares to forward, still no MAC learning) → **15 seconds** →\n3. **Learning** (learns MACs from frames but doesn't forward) → **15 seconds** →\n4. **Forwarding** (fully operational, forwards data and learns MACs).\n\n**Why this gradual transition?** It allows the network to stabilize and ensures all switches have consistent MAC address tables before the newly forwarding port starts passing user traffic. This prevents temporary loops or frame loss during reconvergence.\n\n#### Blocked Ports as Backup Paths\n\nThe key insight: **A blocked port is not broken—it is intentionally disabled to prevent loops, but it remains as a hot standby.** If the current active path (Root Port or Designated Port) fails, STP will:\n1. Stop receiving BPDUs on the failed port.\n2. Promote a blocked port to forwarding state (after the listening/learning delay).\n3. Restore connectivity via the previously redundant path.\n\nThis is why redundant links are valuable in a switched network: STP ensures no loops while still providing automatic failover.",
      "old_string": "### 4. Blocking Port\n- Any port that is **not** a Root Port and **not** a Designated Port is placed in a **Blocking** (or Discarding) state.\n- These ports do not forward user data or learn MAC addresses, effectively breaking the loop while remaining ready to activate if a link fails."
